  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "artery_forceps_section_1",
      "section_title": "Artery forceps",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_1",
              "group_title": "Artery Forceps (Haemostatic Forceps)",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Artery forceps are ring-handled, locking surgical instruments used primarily to clamp blood vessels and achieve haemostasis during surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They are also called haemostatic forceps or haemostats. Despite the name, they can be used on other suitable structures or materials in selected circumstances, depending on their design and the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Category: Clamping and haemostatic instrument.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Primary function: Temporarily occluding a bleeding vessel to control haemorrhage.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Usually stainless steel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Construction: Two ring handles, shanks, a box joint, serrated jaws and a ratchet locking mechanism.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Common types: Straight and curved.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Operation: The jaws close around the target structure and the ratchet maintains the selected closure.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_3",
              "group_title": "How to identify artery forceps in an OSCE",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Look for:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Two finger rings.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A ratchet between the handles that locks the instrument closed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A central box joint.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Long, narrow jaws, often with transverse serrations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Straight or curved jaws, depending on the model.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Model identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is an artery forceps, a locking haemostatic instrument used to clamp blood vessels temporarily to control bleeding. It has ring handles, a ratchet, a box joint and serrated jaws.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Labelling of artery forceps",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The following structures are particularly important for practical examinations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Finger rings"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The rings through which the thumb and a finger are inserted to control the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Ratchet or locking mechanism"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interlocking teeth near the handles that hold the jaws closed at a selected position."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Box joint"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The hinge where the two halves cross and articulate, allowing the jaws to open and close."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Shanks"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The sections between the finger rings and jaws that transmit the closing force."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Jaws and serrations"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The working ends that grip the target. Transverse serrations improve grip and may extend along all or part of the jaws, depending on the pattern."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "6. Tips"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The distal ends of the jaws that engage the vessel or other intended target."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important OSCE distinction: The ratchet locks the instrument; the box joint allows articulation; the serrations provide grip. These three structures have different functions."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_5",
              "group_title": "A. According to jaw shape",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Straight artery forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The jaws follow a straight line."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Clamping accessible bleeding points and handling suitable stay sutures or other materials."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Curved artery forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The jaws curve near the working end."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Reaching vessels or bleeding points where a curved approach provides better access."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_6",
              "group_title": "B. According to size and design",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Spencer Wells forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A common general-purpose haemostatic forceps, available in straight and curved forms. Used to clamp bleeding vessels before definitive haemostasis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Kelly forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly have serrations over the distal portion of the jaws. Used for routine haemostasis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Crile forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly have serrations extending along the full length of the jaw. Used for haemostasis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mosquito forceps"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A smaller, finer haemostatic forceps used for small vessels and delicate operative work. We will discuss this separately in the next relevant instrument section."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These named patterns may differ slightly between manufacturers. In an OSCE, use the visible jaw pattern, size and shape to support identification rather than relying on one feature alone."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Principles of haemostasis using artery forceps",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Haemostasis is the process of stopping bleeding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Artery forceps achieve temporary mechanical haemostasis by compressing a bleeding vessel. Definitive control may then be achieved by ligation, an appropriate energy device or another method selected for the vessel and procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "The basic principles are:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Identify the bleeding point and obtain adequate visualisation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Select a suitable haemostatic instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Securely grasp the bleeding point or vessel using an appropriate technique.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid unnecessarily including surrounding structures in the jaws.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Apply only the required degree of clamping.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain control while the appropriate definitive haemostatic method is performed.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm haemostasis before proceeding.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clinical caution: Artery forceps can damage vessel walls and surrounding tissue. They are not interchangeable with atraumatic vascular clamps designed to preserve the integrity of vessels during vascular reconstruction."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Uses and surgeries",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Specialty or procedure",
                    "Application"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "General surgery",
                      "Temporary control of bleeding vessels during open operations"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hernia repair",
                      "Haemostasis during dissection and wound closure"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Thyroid surgery",
                      "Clamping selected bleeding points, with careful protection of adjacent structures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Breast surgery",
                      "Haemostasis during dissection and excision"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Obstetrics and gynaecology",
                      "Control of suitable bleeding points during open procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Plastic surgery",
                      "Haemostasis during wound exploration and reconstruction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Emergency surgery",
                      "Temporary control of accessible bleeding points during appropriate procedures"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The instrument does not permanently stop bleeding simply by being applied. The surgeon must ensure that definitive haemostasis is achieved when required."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The ratchet maintains closure without continuous hand pressure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Provides temporary control of accessible bleeding vessels.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Available in different sizes and jaw configurations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Straight and curved forms improve access in different operative fields.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not require electricity.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Disadvantages"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive compression can damage vessel walls and surrounding tissues.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect placement may fail to control bleeding.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bulky jaws may be unsuitable for very small vessels or restricted spaces.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It may obstruct the operative field while applied.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "It provides temporary rather than necessarily definitive haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_10",
              "group_title": "Patient-related complications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Persistent bleeding if the vessel is not adequately controlled.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Vessel-wall injury from excessive compression.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Damage to adjacent nerves or other structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Tissue crushing or tearing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ischaemic injury if blood flow to an important structure is compromised for an inappropriate duration.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wound contamination if aseptic technique is breached.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_11",
              "group_title": "Instrument-related problems",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Blunt, damaged or misaligned jaws.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A faulty ratchet that fails to lock or release properly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A loose or defective box joint.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Corrosion or inadequate reprocessing.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Before use, inspect the jaws, joint, ratchet and overall instrument integrity."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Artery forceps versus mosquito forceps versus Kocher forceps",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/artery_forceps_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This comparison is especially important because these instruments may look similar on an OSCE tray."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Artery forceps",
                    "Mosquito forceps",
                    "Kocher forceps"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Main function",
                      "Haemostasis",
                      "Haemostasis of small bleeding points",
                      "Secure grasping and clamping of suitable tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Size",
                      "Usually medium or larger, depending on type",
                      "Small and delicate",
                      "Often robust"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Jaw pattern",
                      "Usually serrated",
                      "Fine, usually serrated",
                      "Serrations plus interlocking teeth at the tip"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Teeth",
                      "Usually absent in standard patterns",
                      "Usually absent",
                      "Typically has 1 × 2 teeth"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Key identifying feature",
                      "Ring handles, ratchet and haemostatic jaws",
                      "Small size and fine jaws",
                      "Teeth at the tips"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield point: A Kocher forceps is distinguished by its toothed tip, whereas standard Spencer Wells, Kelly and Crile patterns generally have serrated jaws without terminal teeth."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Common OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is this instrument?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is an artery forceps, a locking haemostatic instrument used to clamp bleeding vessels temporarily."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is its primary function?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Temporary mechanical haemostasis by compressing a bleeding vessel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the function of the ratchet?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It maintains jaw closure at a selected position without requiring continuous hand pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the function of the box joint?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It connects the two halves and permits controlled opening and closing of the jaws."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why are there straight and curved varieties?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To provide suitable access to bleeding points in different anatomical positions and operative fields."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. How does a Kocher forceps differ from a standard artery forceps?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A typical Kocher forceps has interlocking teeth at its tip, giving it a more secure but potentially more traumatic grip."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What are the complications of artery forceps?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Persistent bleeding, vessel-wall damage, tissue crushing and injury to adjacent structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Is clamping the vessel always definitive haemostasis?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. The clamp often provides temporary control while definitive haemostasis is achieved by ligation, an appropriate energy device or another suitable method."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "artery_forceps_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. High-yield revision card",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "artery_forceps_group_14",
              "group_title": "Artery forceps — must know",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Category: Haemostatic instrument.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Main function: Temporary control of bleeding vessels.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Parts: Finger rings, ratchet, shanks, box joint, jaws and tips.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Types: Straight, curved and several named patterns.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Major comparison: Mosquito forceps are smaller; Kocher forceps have toothed tips.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Major complication: Vessel or adjacent tissue injury from inappropriate clamping.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Safety principle: Apply the appropriate clamp to the intended target and ensure definitive haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**Model 30-second OSCE presentation**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is an artery forceps, also known as a haemostatic forceps. It is used to clamp bleeding vessels temporarily during surgery. It consists of finger rings, a ratchet, shanks, a box joint and serrated jaws. It is available in straight and curved forms. Its main advantage is that the ratchet maintains closure without continuous hand pressure. Complications include vessel-wall damage, tissue trauma and persistent bleeding if haemostasis is inadequate. I would inspect the jaws, joint and ratchet before use.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}